Ze-Reviewed-Against: github.com/vishvananda/netlink v1.3.1

The policy and state readers decode an XFRM address with the family of the
message that carries it. Upstream's XfrmAddress.ToIP (nl/xfrm_linux.go)
takes the IPv4 arm whenever the last twelve bytes are zero, so an IPv6
address such as 2001:db8:: reads back as 32.1.13.184, and parseXfrmPolicy
decodes selector prefixes with the family the caller asked for, which is
zero in an all-family dump, rather than with the selector's own. The
readers now take the selector family for policy prefixes, each template's
own family for its tunnel endpoints, which can differ from the selector
family, the SAD message family for a state's outer addresses, and the
nested selector's family for its prefixes. An all-family dump keeps
0.0.0.0/0 and ::/0 distinct, and an IPv4 address is returned in four bytes
rather than as an IPv4-mapped IPv6 address.

Its tests, in xfrm_readback_family_linux_test.go, parse policy and state
messages built by hand: TestXfrmPolicyReadbackSelectorFamily,
TestXfrmPolicyReadbackTemplateFamily and TestXfrmStateReadbackAddressFamily.

No upstream pull request is opened yet for this change.

Needs netlink-xfrm-01-state-readback-esn-replay.patch: its state hunk
carries a line that patch adds as context.

Applies unchanged to upstream main (e0e26cd, 2026-11-13) after the patches it
needs, not on its own, and its test passes there unprivileged.

Delete this patch when go.mod requires an upstream release that contains the
change.

